{
  "id": "be-am-is-are",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "First words, first sentences",
  "title": "The verb be: am, is, are",
  "tagline": "The most important verb in English: who you are, where you are and how you feel.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Speaking Part 1 — questions about you",
    "Reading Part 1 — signs and messages",
    "Writing Part 6 — a short email"
  ],
  "why": "<i>Be</i> is in almost every conversation: your name, your age, where you are from, how you feel. In Speaking Part 1 the examiner asks <i>Where are you from?</i> and <i>How old are you?</i> — and you answer with <i>be</i>. If you say <i>I have twelve years</i>, everybody understands you, but it is a mistake. Get this verb right and you sound like a real English speaker from your first sentence.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "It's Monday morning. Sofia is at the school gate. <b>Where's</b> Mateo? <b>Is</b> he at home? No — look! He<b>'s</b> here, and he <b>is</b> very late. \"Sorry! I<b>'m</b> tired,\" he says. \"We<b>'re</b> all tired,\" says Nadia. \"It<b>'s</b> Monday!\"",
    "ask": "The verb <i>be</i> has three shapes: <b>am</b>, <b>is</b> and <b>are</b>. Can you find all of them in the story? (Careful — some are hiding inside short forms like <i>he's</i>.)"
  },
